# CLAUDE.md

This file provides guidance to Claude Code (claude.ai/code) when working with code in this repository.

## What this is

aiJudge — an AI-assisted assessment platform for STEM coursework (programming,
mathematics, written reports) on one grading substrate. It is a **uv workspace
modular monolith**: one repo, one deploy unit, strictly enforced module
boundaries.

Read `README.md` for the current build status per phase, and
`docs/design/00_システム設計方針と構築計画.md` §1 for the nine design principles
(P1–P9) that every architectural argument in this codebase cites by number.
`docs/adr/` holds the decisions; comments in the source reference them by number
and expect you to have read them before changing the behaviour they describe.

## Commands

```fish
uv sync --extra dev

uv run pytest                                   # the whole suite
uv run pytest packages/skill/tests/test_skill.py -v      # one file
uv run pytest -k test_late_penalty              # one test by name
uv run ruff check .                             # lint
uv run ruff format .                            # format (line-length 100 — CI checks this)
uv run mypy packages/core/src                   # strict, core only
uv run lint-imports                             # module boundary contracts

uv run alembic upgrade head                     # apply migrations (operational DBs too; never --create-schema)
uv run alembic revision --autogenerate -m "..." # after any change to packages/persistence/.../schema.py
```

`packages/persistence/tests/test_migrations.py` fails the build when the schema
built by migrations alone drifts from the model, so a schema change without a
new revision does not pass CI.

CI (`.github/workflows/ci.yml`) runs exactly those five checks plus an accuracy
job. **Run `ruff format .` from the repo root**, not a subdirectory — the
`line-length = 100` setting lives in the root `pyproject.toml`, and formatting
from elsewhere silently falls back to 88 and breaks the CI format check.

### Processes

```fish
docker compose up -d                            # PostgreSQL + MinIO
uv run aijudge-web --create-schema              # learners, :8080 (first run creates schema)
uv run aijudge-worker --phase deterministic     # fast lane
uv run aijudge-worker --phase ai                # slow lane — separate queue on purpose (ADR 0011)
uv run aijudge-review                           # instructors + /manage, :8765
uv run aijudge-finalize --once                  # close grades past the deadline (cron or resident)
uv run aijudge-admin ...                        # start-of-term bulk ops, user creation (CLI only)
uv run aijudge-eval --subject cs_intro_c --out accuracy.md   # measure agreement; never grades
```

### Tests that are off by default

```fish
AIJUDGE_LIVE_LLM=1 uv run pytest evals/test_llm_live.py -v -s
AIJUDGE_SANDBOX=docker uv run pytest packages/sandbox/tests/test_container.py -v
```

The container suite must **pass, not skip**, before real submissions run through
the system; skip means "unverified", not "safe" (`docs/RUNNING.md`, ADR 0006).
On macOS use colima. `sandbox-exec` alone cannot contain a fork bomb.

### Environment

`AIJUDGE_DATABASE_URL` (SQLite works but has no row locking — one worker only),
`AIJUDGE_LLM_BASE_URL` / `AIJUDGE_LLM_MODEL` (default is a local ollama — a
default must never send learner data off the machine, per P7),
`AIJUDGE_SANDBOX` / `AIJUDGE_SANDBOX_WORKDIR` (must be a path the container can
mount — host temp dirs on macOS bind-mount empty), `AIJUDGE_GOLDEN_DIR`
(observation records live **outside the repo**; they contain student work).

## Architecture

### Layering

`packages/core` is the subject-agnostic domain model and the event contracts. It
depends on nothing and performs **no I/O**. Every other subsystem is a
`packages/*` sibling that may import core and nothing else of ours — subsystems
communicate through `aijudge_core.events`, never by importing each other.
`apps/*` are the only composition roots allowed to combine subsystems.
`packages/persistence` is infrastructure: no subsystem may import it.

These rules are contracts in `.importlinter` and **fail the build**, not the
review (ADR 0001). Adding a `packages/*` or `evaluators/*` directory means
registering it in `.importlinter`; `packages/core/tests/test_boundaries.py`
catches the omission. New test directories also need adding to `testpaths` in the
root `pyproject.toml` — an omission there means the tests silently never run,
which has already happened once.

### Every store and repository has two implementations

Each Store / Repository Protocol in `packages/submission/.../protocols.py` has an
in-memory implementation (`packages/submission/.../memory.py`) and a persistence
implementation (`packages/persistence/.../repositories.py`).
`packages/persistence/tests/test_repositories.py` runs the **same tests against
both** — its `database` fixture is parametrised (SQLite always, plus PostgreSQL
when `AIJUDGE_TEST_DATABASE_URL` is set). Adding a Protocol method means
implementing it in both places; the only place the two legitimately differ is row
locking, which is branched explicitly.

### Subjects are declarations, not code

`packages/grading` never imports an evaluator. Evaluators register through the
`aijudge.evaluators` entry point group (normalizers through
`aijudge.normalizers`), and a profile in `subjects/*.yaml` names which ones run,
in what order, and under what review policy. Naming an uninstalled evaluator
fails at load, not at grading time. Adding a subject must not touch the engine —
adding report grading (Phase 3) changed `core` and `grading` by zero lines, and
that is the standing acceptance test for this design (ADR 0002).

`subjects/*.yaml` is grading configuration, and **a profile any course uses is
read-only in the browser** — one profile is the template for several courses, so
rewriting it changes grading for courses the editor does not teach. `/manage/subjects`
enforces that: a profile referenced by zero courses can be edited and renamed
directly, a referenced one can only be *duplicated* (the copy is unreferenced, so
it is editable, and existing courses keep pointing at the original). The
referenced/unreferenced decision lives in `aijudge_admin.profiles`, not in the
templates, and the checks run again at save time — a screen that only greys the
field out cannot be trusted as the boundary (#146).

The repository's own `subjects/` is **samples** (`subjects/README.md`); operational
profiles live outside the git checkout, at `AIJUDGE_PROFILES_DIR`, or a deploy
would overwrite them. Duplication copies the file **byte for byte** and patches
only `name`/`description`: most of each profile is comments recording *why* a
value is what it is, and round-tripping the model through `yaml.dump` erases all
of it.

Operational values of the same kind as a deadline (grace periods, late-penalty
ladders, course rubrics) live on the course or task and are editable in
`/manage`.

### Deterministic first, AI second (P3)

The pipeline order is deterministic evaluators → AI evaluators → aggregation. A
criterion settled deterministically is never sent to a model. With the AI
evaluators removed grading still completes — that is the degraded-mode
requirement (P2), and when S6 is down the AI criteria record as *unscored* and
**the overall total is withheld** rather than renormalised, because a partial
total reads as a complete one.

AI output is always structured and evidenced: `criterion_id → evidence →
judgement → score → rationale` (P4). Nothing calls a provider directly —
`packages/llm_gateway` enforces the policy that learner data reaches local models
only, validates against the schema, retries with the actual error, and records
prompt version and model id (P7, ADR 0004).

### The distinctions the record keeps apart

Several bugs in this codebase's history were one concept collapsed into another.
Treat these as load-bearing:

- **`HumanReview` vs `Finalization`** — an instructor read *this* submission
  vs. the grade closed by some route. Only the former is evidence for κ. Collapse
  them and a term's measured agreement is invented (ADR 0010).
- **Grading vs measurement** — `import-linter` forbids imports in *both*
  directions. `packages/observation` exists as its own package because the
  record type living in `analytics` meant deleting analytics stopped grading
  (ADR 0005, ADR 0007). Accuracy has three verdicts, and exit code `2`
  (not measurable) is **not a pass**.
- **Lateness vs the rubric** — the pipeline never sees `due_at`. The deduction is
  applied afterwards by `aijudge_core.final_score` from a ladder on the course,
  and is recorded on the run rather than recomputed at display time (ADR 0013).
- **Grading vs reviewing** — the worker grades on submission; the console reads
  what arrived. Reviewing is not a precondition for grading (ADR 0007).
- **`evals/report_ja/` vs production** — that harness reads instructor marking
  sheets and personal data. Its findings are *transcribed* into subject profiles
  and evaluators; its code is never imported.
  `tests/test_evals_stay_out_of_production.py` reads the source to enforce this,
  because import-linter cannot see plain scripts.

### Human-in-the-loop is the default (P5)

AI grading proposes; it does not decide. Confidence, evaluator disagreement, and
boundary-score proximity route to review. Blind marking for measurement is
**sampled** by a hash of the submission id (never the instructor's choice), and
the blind page has the verdict *absent* from the response, not merely hidden — a
test asserts the body is clean.

## Conventions

- Comments and docs are **Japanese**; identifiers are English. Ruff's RUF001–003
  are disabled because they conflict with Japanese punctuation.
- Comments here carry the *reason and the measurement* behind a decision, often
  citing an ADR and the defect that produced the rule. When changing such code,
  update the comment's argument — do not delete it.
- Grading results are append-only; reruns do not overwrite (P8). Reproducibility
  fields `(rubric_version, prompt_version, model_id, params, input_hash)` belong
  on the `GradingRun`.
- Anything that reports a number it cannot justify should report `NOT_MEASURED`
  with its reason instead. This pattern recurs across accuracy gates, approval
  rate, difficulty estimation, and duplicate detection, and is deliberate.
- Test file basenames must be unique across the whole repo — the test
  directories have no `__init__.py`, so two files sharing a name break pytest
  collection for the entire suite, not just those files.
